# {{ name | kebab_case }}

A PostgreSQL for the data of your Axum APIs, generated by the `postgres` Moon
template. It is separate from the PostgreSQL that ZITADEL uses in
`deploy/dev/auth-server`, which belongs to ZITADEL and should not be shared.

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:up      # start it and create the APIs' databases
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:down    # stop it, keeping the data
```

It listens on `127.0.0.1:{{ port }}` (`POSTGRES_PORT` in `.env.example`; Moon checks it
against the other services' ports) and keeps its data in a Docker volume named
after this repository folder, so several projects on one machine stay apart.
`docker compose down -v` in this folder deletes the data.

## One database per API

An API generated with a database (`moon generate axum -- --name ads --port 4001 --database`)
ships `db/init.sql`, which creates a login role and a database named after the
API, with local-only passwords. `{{ name | kebab_case }}:up` applies every
`services/*/db/init.sql` each time it runs, and the files only create what is
missing, so adding an API later needs no reset. A service can reach only its own
database, and its role has `CREATEDB` so `cargo test` can create a throwaway
database per test.

The API reads the connection string from `DATABASE_URL` in its `.env` (the
example file has the local one).

## Production

Run PostgreSQL where you can back it up, ideally a managed service, and give
each API its own database and role with a strong password. The passwords in
`db/init.sql` are for local development only. Set `DATABASE_URL` on the API's
container, and read the API's README about connecting over TLS: the default
build connects in plain text, which is right on a private network and wrong
over the internet.
